WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.511
Blazor is component-based, but
component-based does not mean security is automatic.

2
00:00:06.591 --> 00:00:11.366
Components can make a user interface
easier to compose and maintain,

3
00:00:11.446 --> 00:00:16.798
but the important security question is
still where trust decisions are made.

4
00:00:16.878 --> 00:00:22.230
A component may display data, collect
input, call an API, hold browser state,

5
00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:25.521
or participate in a state-changing
workflow.

6
00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:30.377
Blazor applications may execute logic on
the server, in the browser,

7
00:00:30.457 --> 00:00:34.491
or across both depending on hosting model
and render mode.

8
00:00:34.571 --> 00:00:39.100
That means a secure design starts by
locating the trust boundary.

9
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.861
Code that runs on the server can enforce
decisions with access to trusted services

10
00:00:44.941 --> 00:00:47.082
and protected configuration.

11
00:00:47.162 --> 00:00:52.843
Code delivered to the browser must be
treated as visible to and controllable by the

12
00:00:52.923 --> 00:00:53.335
user.

13
00:00:53.415 --> 00:00:59.178
Browser-executed code should not contain
secrets, trusted authorization logic,

14
00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:02.716
or assumptions that only the server can
validate.

15
00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:07.243
It can guide the experience, collect
input, render components,

16
00:01:07.323 --> 00:01:09.381
and call protected services.

17
00:01:09.461 --> 00:01:15.307
It cannot become the final authority for
access to protected data or important state

18
00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:16.046
changes.

19
00:01:16.126 --> 00:01:20.407
A secure Blazor baseline includes explicit
authentication,

20
00:01:20.487 --> 00:01:23.698
server or API authorization, API
protection,

21
00:01:23.778 --> 00:01:27.895
validation at trusted boundaries, careful
browser state,

22
00:01:27.975 --> 00:01:34.068
reviewed JavaScript interop, safe
rendering, useful logging, and deployment review.

23
00:01:34.148 --> 00:01:37.771
These are ordinary engineering
responsibilities,

24
00:01:37.851 --> 00:01:41.062
not features added after the component
works.

25
00:01:41.142 --> 00:01:45.177
The practical goal is to make security
decisions visible.

26
00:01:45.257 --> 00:01:49.703
Reviewers should be able to tell where
identity is established,

27
00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:53.982
where authorization is enforced, where
input is validated,

28
00:01:54.062 --> 00:01:58.343
what data is sent to the browser, what
state is stored locally,

29
00:01:58.423 --> 00:02:03.940
and what behavior depends on JavaScript
interop or production configuration.
